  "state": {
    "case_id": "5",
    "case_summary": "A 19-year-old male, unemployed, residing in Angelopolis (Antioquia, 6.06°N 75.42°W), a non-malarious area at an altitude of 1,900 meters above sea level, visited the emergency department on 10th April 2001. He stayed there for 24 hours before being moved to the Internal Medicine Ward. On 23rd April, he exhibited fever and was treated with phenytoin, dipyrone, ampicillin, ranitidine, ceftriaxone, methylprednisolone, oxacillin, amikacin, and enoxaparin (administered via a heparin lock). Additionally, he received chloroquine 150 mg daily starting from 24th April 2001. He was discharged on 19th May 2001. On 6th June 2001, he was readmitted with a febrile syndrome.\n\nA thick smear revealed the presence of 14,440 asexual forms per μl in peripheral blood.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
